<!-- §01 Memory Squeeze -->
<section id="s-squeeze" data-code="squeeze">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Memory Squeeze</div>
<h2>A fixed-size state must store an unbounded history</h2>
<p>Transformer attention gives every token direct access to every past token via an explicit key-value cache. Elegant — but the cache grows linearly with sequence length, making long-context inference expensive.</p>
<p>Linear attention trades that unbounded cache for a fixed-size recurrent state S_t ∈ R<sup>d_k &times; d_v</sup>. Every new token writes its key-value association into S by adding an outer product:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Basic Linear Attention</div>
  <span class="math-var">S</span><sub>t</sub> = <span class="math-var">S</span><sub>t−1</sub> + <span class="math-var">k</span><sub>t</sub> <span class="math-var">v</span><sub>t</sub><sup>⊤</sup>,&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-var">o</span><sub>t</sub> = <span class="math-var">S</span><sub>t</sub><sup>⊤</sup> <span class="math-var">q</span><sub>t</sub>
  <div class="math-comment" style="margin-top:.5rem">State has fixed size regardless of sequence length — no memory growth</div>
</div>

<p>The constraint is immediate: the state has constant size, but the sequence keeps growing. Every new outer product is added to a bucket that never empties. Old associations are never removed — they accumulate until new writes overwhelm them through superposition.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">association_collision.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Click to simulate writes</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="collision-canvas" style="width:100%;height:200px;display:block;border-radius:6px;background:var(--card2)"></canvas>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.5rem;margin-top:.875rem;flex-wrap:wrap">
      <button class="gate-btn" id="col-btn-paris1">t=1: Write "Paris→city"</button>
      <button class="gate-btn" id="col-btn-rome">t=3: Write "Rome→cuisine"</button>
      <button class="gate-btn" id="col-btn-paris2">t=9: Write "Paris→fashion"</button>
      <button class="gate-btn" id="col-btn-query">Query "Paris" →</button>
      <button class="gate-btn" id="col-btn-reset" style="margin-left:auto">Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="gate-caption">Left: additive writes — "Paris→city" and "Paris→fashion" both persist, retrieval returns a confused mix. Right: delta rule — second "Paris" write first erases the old entry, then commits the new value cleanly.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The delta rule (DeltaNet, 2024) tackled exactly this problem: before writing a new value, first <em>read and subtract</em> whatever is currently stored at that key. This targeted overwrite is far more expressive than simple addition — but it introduced a new constraint that Gated DeltaNet-2 now resolves.</p>
</section>

<!-- §02 Delta Rule Heritage -->
<section id="s-heritage" data-code="heritage">
<div class="section-no">02 — The Delta Rule Heritage</div>
<h2>From additive updates to targeted memory editing</h2>
<p>The progress from basic linear attention to Gated DeltaNet-2 is a story of incrementally tightening control over the state update. Each generation fixed a specific bottleneck while keeping the chunkwise training structure intact.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">model_evolution.navigator</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Step through the family tree</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="evo-steps">
      <div class="step-box active" id="evo-step-0">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Basic Linear Attention</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Accumulates key-value outer products. Old associations never expire — interference grows without bound.</div>
          <div class="step-code">S_t = S_&#123;t-1&#125; + k_t v_t^T</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" id="evo-step-1">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">DeltaNet — Targeted Overwrite</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Subtracts the current read before writing. Scalar β_t ∈ [0,1] controls step size. β=1 fully overwrites the association at key k_t.</div>
          <div class="step-code">S_t = (I − β_t k_t k_t^T) S_&#123;t-1&#125; + β_t k_t v_t^T</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" id="evo-step-2">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Gated DeltaNet — Global Forgetting</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Adds a scalar decay α_t ∈ (0,1] that uniformly fades the entire state. The delta edit then works on the decayed state.</div>
          <div class="step-code">S_t = α_t (I − β_t k_t k_t^T) S_&#123;t-1&#125; + β_t k_t v_t^T</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" id="evo-step-3">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">KDA — Channel-Wise Decay</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Upgrades scalar α_t to a channel vector α_t ∈ (0,1]^d_k. Each key dimension now decays at its own learned rate. β_t remains a scalar.</div>
          <div class="step-code">S_t = (I − β_t k_t k_t^T) Diag(α_t) S_&#123;t-1&#125; + β_t k_t v_t^T</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" id="evo-step-4">
        <div class="step-num">5</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Gated DeltaNet-2 — Decoupled Gates</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Replaces the scalar β_t with two independent channel vectors: erase gate b_t ∈ [0,1]^d_k and write gate w_t ∈ [0,1]^d_v. This is the key contribution.</div>
          <div class="step-code">S_t = (I − k_t (b_t⊙k_t)^T) Diag(α_t) S_&#123;t-1&#125; + k_t (w_t⊙v_t)^T</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="evo-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
      <button id="evo-next">Next →</button>
      <span class="step-prog" id="evo-prog">Step 1 of 5</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>KDA was the direct predecessor: it solved the coarse-grained decay problem by making α_t channel-wise. But the active delta gate — the one responsible for targeted reads and writes — was still a single scalar β_t. That remained the binding constraint through the entire DeltaNet family until Gated DeltaNet-2.</p>

<h3>The fast-weight interpretation</h3>
<p>Each delta-rule update can be viewed as one gradient step on a local regression loss: find S_t that stays close to the decayed prior state while writing the target value at the current key. This "fast-weight" perspective unifies DeltaNet, Gated DeltaNet, KDA, and GDN-2 as online optimizers with progressively more expressive loss terms.</p>
</section>

<!-- §03 The Scalar Tie -->
<section id="s-tie" data-code="tie">
<div class="section-no">03 — The Scalar Tie</div>
<h2>One scalar, two different decisions</h2>
<p>In KDA, the scalar β_t drives two fundamentally different operations simultaneously:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Erasing</strong> — a key-side operation that reads old content from S along the current key direction and subtracts it. Controls which key channels get cleaned up.</li>
  <li><strong>Writing</strong> — a value-side operation that commits a new value into S. Controls which value channels receive new content.</li>
</ul>
<p>These axes are orthogonal. Erasing is about selectively zeroing out old associations at specific key directions. Writing is about inserting new content along specific value dimensions. There is no reason they should share the same magnitude, let alone the same channel structure.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">scalar_tie.canvas — KDA (before)</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">β_t forces both gates to be equal</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="gate-demo">
      <div class="gate-panel">
        <div class="gate-panel-title erase">Key-Side Erase  [ b_t = β_t · 1 ]</div>
        <div class="gate-bars" id="tie-erase-bars">
          <!-- bars injected by JS -->
        </div>
        <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;margin-top:4px">
          <span style="font-size:.6rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">k₁</span>
          <span style="font-size:.6rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">k₂</span>
          <span style="font-size:.6rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">k₃</span>
          <span style="font-size:.6rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">k₄</span>
          <span style="font-size:.6rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">k₅</span>
          <span style="font-size:.6rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">k₆</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="gate-panel">
        <div class="gate-panel-title write">Value-Side Write  [ w_t = β_t · 1 ]</div>
        <div class="gate-bars" id="tie-write-bars">
          <!-- bars injected by JS -->
        </div>
        <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;margin-top:4px">
          <span style="font-size:.6rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">v₁</span>
          <span style="font-size:.6rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">v₂</span>
          <span style="font-size:.6rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">v₃</span>
          <span style="font-size:.6rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">v₄</span>
          <span style="font-size:.6rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">v₅</span>
          <span style="font-size:.6rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">v₆</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="gate-controls">
      <span style="font-size:.7rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted);margin-right:.25rem">β_t =</span>
      <button class="gate-btn" id="tie-btn-01">0.1</button>
      <button class="gate-btn active" id="tie-btn-05">0.5</button>
      <button class="gate-btn" id="tie-btn-09">0.9</button>
    </div>
    <p class="gate-caption">Both gates always have identical values — β_t = 0.5 means both key-side erase and value-side write are 50%. There's no way to erase aggressively (high b) while writing conservatively (low w), or vice versa.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>This scalar tie is not a mathematical necessity — it's a modeling restriction. The state matrix S has distinct key (row) and value (column) axes. A key-side erase selectively removes content by reading along key direction k_t, while a value-side write commits content along value direction v_t. These live on different axes of the same matrix and naturally call for independent control.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">KDA Update (scalar tie)</div>
  <span class="math-var">S</span><sub>t</sub> = (I − <span class="math-var">β</span><sub>t</sub> <span class="math-var">k</span><sub>t</sub> <span class="math-var">k</span><sub>t</sub><sup>⊤</sup>) <span class="math-var">D</span><sub>t</sub> <span class="math-var">S</span><sub>t−1</sub> + <span class="math-var">β</span><sub>t</sub> <span class="math-var">k</span><sub>t</sub> <span class="math-var">v</span><sub>t</sub><sup>⊤</sup>
  <div class="math-comment">Single scalar β_t controls both: (I − β_t k_t k_t^T) erase factor and β_t v_t write factor</div>
</div>
</section>

<!-- §04 Gated Delta Rule-2 -->
<section id="s-decouple" data-code="decouple">
<div class="section-no">04 — Gated Delta Rule-2</div>
<h2>Two gates, two axes</h2>
<p>Gated DeltaNet-2 replaces the single β_t with two independent channel-wise vectors:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>b_t ∈ [0,1]<sup>d_k</sup></strong> — the erase gate. Scales the key direction used to read and remove old content. Lives on the key axis.</li>
  <li><strong>w_t ∈ [0,1]<sup>d_v</sup></strong> — the write gate. Scales the value being committed to the state. Lives on the value axis.</li>
</ul>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Gated Delta Rule-2</div>
  <span class="math-var">e</span><sub>t</sub> = <span class="math-var">b</span><sub>t</sub> ⊙ <span class="math-var">k</span><sub>t</sub>,&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-var">z</span><sub>t</sub> = <span class="math-var">w</span><sub>t</sub> ⊙ <span class="math-var">v</span><sub>t</sub><br>
  <span class="math-var">S</span><sub>t</sub> = (I − <span class="math-var">k</span><sub>t</sub> <span class="math-var">e</span><sub>t</sub><sup>⊤</sup>) <span class="math-var">D</span><sub>t</sub> <span class="math-var">S</span><sub>t−1</sub> + <span class="math-var">k</span><sub>t</sub> <span class="math-var">z</span><sub>t</sub><sup>⊤</sup>
  <div class="math-comment">D_t = Diag(α_t) is the channel-wise decay from KDA. b_t and w_t are independent projections of x_t through sigmoid.</div>
</div>

<p>The key insight: b_t selectively weights <em>which key channels</em> the erase operation reads from, while w_t independently weights <em>which value channels</em> the write operation commits to. They are produced by entirely separate linear projections:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <span class="math-var">b</span><sub>t</sub> = σ(<span class="math-var">W</span><sub>b</sub> <span class="math-var">x</span><sub>t</sub>),&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-var">w</span><sub>t</sub> = σ(<span class="math-var">W</span><sub>w</sub> <span class="math-var">x</span><sub>t</sub>)
</div>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">decoupled_gates.canvas — GDN-2 (after)</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Gates now move independently</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="gate-demo">
      <div class="gate-panel">
        <div class="gate-panel-title erase">Erase Gate b_t ∈ [0,1]^&#123;d_k&#125;</div>
        <div class="gate-bars" id="dec-erase-bars"></div>
        <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;margin-top:4px">
          <span style="font-size:.6rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">k₁</span>
          <span style="font-size:.6rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">k₂</span>
          <span style="font-size:.6rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">k₃</span>
          <span style="font-size:.6rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">k₄</span>
          <span style="font-size:.6rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">k₅</span>
          <span style="font-size:.6rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">k₆</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="gate-panel">
        <div class="gate-panel-title write">Write Gate w_t ∈ [0,1]^&#123;d_v&#125;</div>
        <div class="gate-bars" id="dec-write-bars"></div>
        <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;margin-top:4px">
          <span style="font-size:.6rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">v₁</span>
          <span style="font-size:.6rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">v₂</span>
          <span style="font-size:.6rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">v₃</span>
          <span style="font-size:.6rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">v₄</span>
          <span style="font-size:.6rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">v₅</span>
          <span style="font-size:.6rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">v₆</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="gate-controls">
      <span style="font-size:.7rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted);margin-right:.25rem">Preset:</span>
      <button class="gate-btn active" id="dec-btn-asym">Asymmetric</button>
      <button class="gate-btn" id="dec-btn-erase-broad">Erase broad / Write narrow</button>
      <button class="gate-btn" id="dec-btn-no-erase">Skip erase / Full write</button>
      <button class="gate-btn" id="dec-btn-tied">Tied (= KDA)</button>
    </div>
    <p class="gate-caption" id="dec-caption">Asymmetric: b_t erases key channels 1,3,5 strongly while w_t writes to value channels 2,4,6. Impossible with a single scalar β_t.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>Special cases recover prior models</h3>
<p>The generalization is clean: if b_t = β_t · 1<sub>d_k</sub> and w_t = β_t · 1<sub>d_v</sub> (both collapse to the same scalar), Gated Delta Rule-2 exactly recovers KDA. If the channel-wise decay α_t is further collapsed to a scalar, it recovers Gated DeltaNet. Both prior models are tied subspaces of the more general update.</p>

<p>The ablation results in the paper confirm that both gates contribute independently — removing channel-wise structure from either one hurts performance, with the erase gate b_t accounting for most of the gain. This matches the intuition that controlling <em>which key channels get erased</em> is the binding bottleneck for multi-association memory.</p>
</section>

