<h3>Chunkwise WY training</h3>
<p>The channel-wise decay α_t creates a complication: it enters the recurrence as Diag(α_t), meaning each key channel has its own cumulative product. Naïvely, this prevents the tidy parallel structure used in DeltaNet's WY chunkwise algorithm.</p>
<p>The solution: absorb the cumulative decay γ_r = Π α_i into the erase and key factors. Define decay-normalized vectors k̄_r = γ_r<sup>-1</sup> ⊙ k_r and ē_r = γ_r ⊙ (b_r ⊙ k_r). The recurrence in these normalized coordinates has exactly the same triangular WY form as before — channel-wise decay disappears from the recurrence itself and reappears only in the erase factors.</p>
<p>The result: same Triton kernel structure as KDA, with only a small constant overhead for the two separate erase/write gate projections. Training throughput on a single H100 drops from ~38 Kt/s (KDA) to ~36 Kt/s (GDN-2) — a modest 5% cost for the added expressiveness.</p>
</section>

