<h3>Why multi-key retrieval shows the biggest gain</h3>
<p>The MK-NIAH (Multi-Key Needle-In-A-Haystack) task requires the model to retrieve a value associated with a specific key when multiple similar keys exist in the context — exactly the interference scenario that decoupled gating was designed to address. With a scalar β_t, any attempt to sharpen the erase of one key channel equally sharpens (or blunts) the write to all value channels. With b_t and w_t decoupled, the model can learn to be precise on the key side without sacrificing flexibility on the value side.</p>

<h3>Real-world retrieval</h3>
<p>The gains extend to realistic tasks. On a suite of real-world recall benchmarks (SWDE, SQuAD, FDA, TriviaQA, NQ, DROP), Gated DeltaNet-2 achieves 29.88 average in the recurrent setting versus 28.67 for KDA and 28.09 for Gated DeltaNet — consistent improvement across both structured extraction and open-domain QA.</p>

